(aggregator name vs merchant name, zero common prefix), and Amazon Mktplace/-30.81 (4-day gap,
outside the 3-day window). Every one of these misses is a direct, mechanical consequence of the
exact constants the spec dictates (pendingPostedWindowDays=3, pendingPostedPrefixMinLen=12,
Description-only HasPrefix/common-prefix logic) — the implementation matches the spec's own
algorithm verbatim, and A2's "exactly 11" was itself calibrated by the spec author running this
same algorithm against live data (DP-RUN-SPEC.md A2 note). Not a FAIL: no acceptance criterion
promises catching every true duplicate, and the spec explicitly owns this precision/recall
tradeoff. Backlog observation only.

Attack 2 — greedy-pairing "steal": built a synthetic 3-txn fixture (posted "Check #123", a
shape-1 bill-pay candidate 2 days out, and a shape-3 pending candidate 1 day out sharing a prefix
with the check description). Confirmed the shape-3 candidate wins the greedy smallest-date-diff
selection and the shape-1 candidate is left unpaired (1 pair formed, not the 2 that existed in the
2-txn-only case). This is real but is a property of the pre-existing, spec-frozen greedy 1:1
pairing loop ("No change to... the greedy pairing loop" — DP-RUN-SPEC.md "Explicitly out of
scope") — shapes 1 and 2 could already steal from each other before DP1; adding a third shape only
adds more candidates into the same unmodified mechanism. A2's real-data check is the actual test
for whether this bites the 6 live decisions, and it passes (5 resolved + 1 kept_both bind).
Backlog observation, not a FAIL.

